    "summary_60s": "Scalars have magnitude only (distance, speed, mass, energy, potential). Vectors have magnitude AND direction (displacement, velocity, force, momentum, electric field). Resultant of two perpendicular vectors is R = √(A² + B²). Relative velocity of A relative to B is V_AB = V_A - V_B.",
    "key_formulas": "R² = P² + Q² + 2PQ cos θ\ntan α = (Q sin θ) / (P + Q cos θ)\nPerpendicular: R = √(Rx² + Ry²), tan θ = Ry / Rx\nRx = R cos θ, Ry = R sin θ\nV_AB = V_A - V_B",
    "pro_tips_95": "Work, Electric Potential, Pressure, and Temperature are SCALARS even though pressure has direction and work involves displacement! In relative velocity, when two bodies move towards each other, relative speed is the SUM (V_A + V_B). When in same direction, it is the DIFFERENCE (V_A - V_B)!",
    "syllabus_objectives": "Candidates should be able to: 1. Distinguish between scalar and vector quantities with standard examples. 2. Determine resultant of vectors using triangle and parallelogram laws. 3. Resolve vectors into rectangular components. 4. Solve authentic problems on relative velocity.",
    "updated_at": "Official 2026/2027 Syllabus Masterclass",
    "content": "# SCALARS AND VECTORS\n\n## Distinction Between Scalar and Vector Quantities\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Define scalar and vector quantities with precise physical distinction.\n> - Correctly classify physical quantities tested in JAMB and WAEC.\n\n### 1. Fundamental Definitions\n- **Scalar Quantity:** A physical quantity that is completely specified by its **magnitude (numerical value and appropriate unit)** alone, without reference to direction. Scalars obey ordinary rules of scalar algebra ($2\\text{ kg} + 3\\text{ kg} = 5\\text{ kg}$).\n- **Vector Quantity:** A physical quantity that possesses **both magnitude and a definite direction in space**, and obeys specific laws of vector addition (Triangle or Parallelogram law).\n\n### 2. High-Yield Examination Classification\n\n| Physical Quantity | Classification | Common Trap Reason / Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Speed** | Scalar | Rate of change of distance; no direction |\n| **Velocity** | Vector | Rate of change of displacement in a specified direction |\n| **Distance** | Scalar | Actual ground length covered |\n| **Displacement** | Vector | Straight-line distance from start to finish with direction |\n| **Mass** | Scalar | Invariable quantity of matter |\n| **Weight** | Vector | Gravitational force acting downwards toward Earth's core |\n| **Pressure** | **SCALAR** | **EXAMINER TRAP:** Acts equally in all directions at a point in fluid |\n| **Work & Energy** | **SCALAR** | **EXAMINER TRAP:** Dot product of two vectors ($\\vec{F} \\cdot \\vec{s}$); scalar |\n| **Electric Current** | **SCALAR** | Has direction along wire, but **does NOT obey vector addition** |\n| **Momentum** | Vector | Product of mass (scalar) and velocity (vector) |\n\n---\n\n## Vector Addition: Triangle and Parallelogram Laws\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - State the Triangle Law and Parallelogram Law of vectors.\n> - Calculate the resultant of two vectors inclined at an arbitrary angle $\\theta$.\n\n### 1. Parallelogram Law of Vectors\nIf two vectors are represented in magnitude and direction by the two adjacent sides of a parallelogram drawn from a common point, their **Resultant ($R$)** is completely represented by the diagonal passing through that point.\n\n$$R = \\sqrt{P^2 + Q^2 + 2PQ \\cos \\theta}$$\n\n- **Special Cases:**\n  1. Two vectors in the same direction ($\\theta = 0^\\circ$): $R_{\\max} = P + Q$.\n  2. Two vectors in opposite directions ($\\theta = 180^\\circ$): $R_{\\min} = |P - Q|$.\n  3. Two vectors at right angles ($\\theta = 90^\\circ$): $R = \\sqrt{P^2 + Q^2}$.\n\n---\n\n## Resolution of Vectors into Perpendicular Components\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Resolve any vector inclined at angle $\\theta$ into horizontal and vertical components.\n> - Determine the resultant of multiple coplanar forces.\n\nGiven a vector $\\vec{R}$ making an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal ($x$-axis):\n- Horizontal Component: $R_x = R \\cos \\theta$\n- Vertical Component: $R_y = R \\sin \\theta$\n\nFor multiple concurrent forces $\\vec{F}_1, \\vec{F}_2, \\ldots$:\n1. Calculate $\\sum F_x = F_{1x} + F_{2x} + \\ldots$\n2. Calculate $\\sum F_y = F_{1y} + F_{2y} + \\ldots$\n3. Net Resultant Magnitude: $R = \\sqrt{(\\sum F_x)^2 + (\\sum F_y)^2}$\n4. Direction: $\\tan \\alpha = \\frac{|\\sum F_y|}{|\\sum F_x|}$\n"
