    "summary_60s": "General form: ax² + bx + c = 0 (a ≠ 0). Discriminant Δ = b² - 4ac dictates root nature: Δ > 0 (two distinct real roots), Δ = 0 (two equal real roots), Δ < 0 (imaginary/no real roots). Sum of roots α + β = -b/a, product αβ = c/a.",
    "key_formulas": "x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / (2a)\nΔ = b² - 4ac\nα + β = -b / a\nαβ = c / a\nEquation: x² - (sum)x + (product) = 0",
    "pro_tips_95": "In JAMB questions asking for α² + β², always rewrite as (α + β)² - 2αβ! For 1/α + 1/β, rewrite as (α + β) / (αβ)! Never attempt to find α and β individually!",
    "syllabus_objectives": "Candidates should be able to: 1. Solve quadratic equations by factorization, completing the square, and quadratic formula. 2. Form quadratic equations given the roots or symmetric functions of roots. 3. Use the discriminant to analyze nature of roots. 4. Sketch and interpret quadratic parabola graphs.",
    "updated_at": "Official 2026/2027 Syllabus Masterclass",
    "content": "# QUADRATIC EQUATIONS AND FUNCTIONS\n\n## Solution Methods: Factorization, Completing the Square & Formula\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Identify standard quadratic form $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ ($a \\neq 0$).\n> - Solve by factorization, completing the square, and general formula.\n\nA second-degree polynomial equation in one variable has the canonical form:\n$$ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\quad (a \\neq 0)$$\n\n### The General Quadratic Formula (Al-Khwarizmi Formula)\n$$x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}$$\n\n---\n\n## Nature of Roots and the Discriminant ($\\Delta$)\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Calculate the discriminant $\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$.\n> - State the conditions for real, equal, distinct, and complex roots.\n\nThe expression under the radical sign is the **Discriminant ($\\Delta$)**:\n$$\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac$$\n\n| Value of $\\Delta$ | Nature of Roots | Parabola Graph Intersection with $x$-axis |\n|---|---|---|\n| **$\\Delta > 0$ and a perfect square** | Real, rational, and unequal (distinct) | Cuts $x$-axis at 2 distinct rational points |\n| **$\\Delta > 0$ and NOT a perfect square** | Real, irrational (surd conjugates), and unequal | Cuts $x$-axis at 2 distinct irrational points |\n| **$\\Delta = 0$** | Real and equal (repeated / coincident root) | Tangent to (touches) $x$-axis at exactly 1 point |\n| **$\\Delta < 0$** | Complex / Imaginary (no real roots) | Does NOT touch or intersect $x$-axis at all |\n\n---\n\n## Symmetric Functions of Roots ($\\alpha$ and $\\beta$)\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Express sum and product of roots in terms of coefficients: $\\alpha + \\beta = -\\frac{b}{a}$, $\\alpha\\beta = \\frac{c}{a}$.\n> - Form new equations whose roots are functions of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$.\n\nIf $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are the roots of $ax^2 + bx + c = 0$:\n$$\\alpha + \\beta = -\\frac{b}{a}, \\quad \\alpha\\beta = \\frac{c}{a}$$\n\n### Standard Algebraic Identities for JAMB:\n1. **Sum of Squares:**\n$$\\alpha^2 + \\beta^2 = (\\alpha + \\beta)^2 - 2\\alpha\\beta$$\n2. **Difference of Roots:**\n$$(\\alpha - \\beta)^2 = (\\alpha + \\beta)^2 - 4\\alpha\\beta \\implies \\alpha - \\beta = \\sqrt{(\\alpha + \\beta)^2 - 4\\alpha\\beta}$$\n3. **Sum of Reciprocals:**\n$$\\frac{1}{\\alpha} + \\frac{1}{\\beta} = \\frac{\\alpha + \\beta}{\\alpha\\beta}$$\n4. **Sum of Cubes:**\n$$\\alpha^3 + \\beta^3 = (\\alpha + \\beta)^3 - 3\\alpha\\beta(\\alpha + \\beta)$$\n"
