    "summary_60s": "Cell theory by Schleiden & Schwann: all living things are composed of cells, the cell is the structural unit of life, all cells arise from pre-existing cells. Mitochondria generate ATP on cristae folds; ribosomes synthesize proteins; chloroplasts carry out photosynthesis in thylakoids.",
    "key_formulas": "Total Magnification = Eyepiece Magnification × Objective Magnification\nActual Size = Image Size / Magnification\n1 mm = 1,000 µm",
    "pro_tips_95": "Viruses are ACELLULAR (non-cellular) and lack cytoplasm or organelles! Plant cells in hypotonic solutions become TURGID (cell wall prevents bursting); animal cells undergo LYSIS (bursting) due to lack of cellulose cell wall!",
    "syllabus_objectives": "Candidates should be able to: 1. State the cell theory and recognize exceptions. 2. Distinguish between prokaryotes and eukaryotes. 3. Differentiate between plant and animal cells. 4. Describe structure and functions of cell organelles. 5. Explain cellular processes of osmosis, diffusion, and plasmolysis.",
    "updated_at": "Official 2026/2027 Syllabus Masterclass",
    "content": "# CELL STRUCTURE AND FUNCTIONS OF CELL ORGANELLES\n\n## The Cell Theory and Classification of Cells\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - State the three tenets of the Classical Cell Theory.\n> - Identify organisms that constitute exceptions to the cell theory (e.g. Viruses).\n> - Contrast Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic cell organization.\n\n### 1. The Classical Cell Theory\nFormulated by **Matthias Schleiden (1838)**, **Theodor Schwann (1839)**, and expanded by **Rudolf Virchow (1855)**:\n1. **All living organisms are composed of one or more cells.**\n2. **The cell is the basic structural, physiological, and functional unit of life.**\n3. **All living cells arise only from pre-existing cells through cell division (*Omnis cellula e cellula*).**\n\n> ⚠️ **WAEC & JAMB Chief Examiner Trap: Exceptions to Cell Theory**\n> - **Viruses:** Acellular entities lacking cytoplasm, membranes, and metabolic enzymes. They are inert chemical crystals outside living host cells.\n> - **Coenocytic / Siphonaceous Organisms:** Fungal hyphae (*Rhizopus*) and algae (*Vaucheria*) consist of continuous multinucleate protoplasmic masses without cellular partitioning.\n> - **Mammalian Mature Red Blood Cells (Erythrocytes):** Function without a nucleus.\n\n---\n\n## Plant Cells vs Animal Cells\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Compare cellular anatomy of typical plant and animal cells under electron microscopy.\n> - Explain the biological significance of cell walls and large central vacuoles in plants.\n\n| Feature | Plant Cell | Animal Cell |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cell Wall** | Rigid outer wall made of **cellulose** present | Absent; enclosed only by flexible plasma membrane |\n| **Chloroplasts** | Present in photosynthetic cells (contain chlorophyll) | Completely absent |\n| **Vacuole** | **Large, single central vacuole** filled with cell sap | Small, temporary, or absent; multiple if present |\n| **Centrioles & Centrosomes** | Absent in higher plants | **Present** (organize spindle fibres during mitosis) |\n| **Shape** | Regular, rigid, definite polygonal shape | Flexible, irregular, variable shape |\n| **Food Storage** | Stored primarily as **Starch grains** | Stored primarily as **Glycogen granules** |\n\n---\n\n## Organelles & Their Specialized Functions\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Identify organelles: Mitochondria, Ribosomes, Endoplasmic Reticulum, Golgi apparatus, Lysosomes, and Chloroplasts.\n> - Relate organelle ultrastructure to cellular metabolic processes.\n\n1. **Mitochondrion (\"Powerhouse of the Cell\"):**\n   - Double-membraned organelle with inner folds called **cristae** that maximize surface area for electron transport chain enzymes.\n   - Matrix contains Krebs cycle enzymes, 70S ribosomes, and circular mitochondrial DNA.\n   - Synthesizes adenosine triphosphate (**ATP**) via aerobic cellular respiration.\n\n2. **Ribosomes (\"Protein Factories\"):**\n   - Non-membrane bound ribonucleoprotein complexes made of rRNA and proteins.\n   - Synthesize polypeptide chains (proteins) during translation.\n\n3. **Endoplasmic Reticulum (ER):**\n   - **Rough ER:** Studded with ribosomes; synthesizes and transports proteins.\n   - **Smooth ER:** Lacks ribosomes; synthesizes lipids, steroids, and detoxifies toxins.\n\n4. **Golgi Apparatus / Dictyosome:**\n   - Flattened stacks of membrane-bound cisternae.\n   - Modifies, sorts, packages, and secretes cellular products (glycoproteins, enzymes).\n\n5. **Lysosomes (\"Suicide Bags\"):**\n   - Membrane-bound vesicles containing powerful hydrolytic digestive enzymes (acid hydrolases).\n   - Digest worn-out organelles (autophagy) and foreign pathogens.\n"
