    "summary_60s": "Concord means harmony between words in person and number. Singular subject takes singular verb (-s/es); plural subject takes plural verb. Parenthetical expressions (as well as, along with, in addition to) DO NOT change subject number. Either...or / Neither...nor obey proximity (agrees with nearest subject).",
    "key_formulas": "Subject + \"as well as\" + [phrase] -> Verb agrees with 1st subject\nEither A or B -> Verb agrees with B (Proximity)\nNeither A nor B -> Verb agrees with B (Proximity)\nMany a + [singular noun] -> Singular verb\nOne of the + [plural noun] + [relative pronoun] -> Plural verb",
    "pro_tips_95": "\"Police\", \"Cattle\", \"Poultry\", and \"People\" are ALWAYS PLURAL nouns and take plural verbs (\"The police are investigations\")! \"News\", \"Mathematics\", \"Physics\", and \"Civics\" are SINGULAR despite ending in -s!",
    "syllabus_objectives": "Candidates should be able to: 1. Apply grammatical rules of subject-verb concord in sentence construction. 2. Resolve agreement problems involving correlative conjunctions (either/or, neither/nor). 3. Identify parenthetical interveners and maintain agreement with true subject. 4. Master indefinite pronouns and notional concord.",
    "updated_at": "Official 2026/2027 Syllabus Masterclass",
    "content": "# CONCORD AND GRAMMATICAL AGREEMENT\n\n## Core Subject-Verb Agreement Principles\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Apply the cardinal rule: Singular subject takes a singular verb; Plural subject takes a plural verb.\n> - Recognize that singular verbs in present tense end in **-s / -es** (runs, plays, writes), whereas singular nouns do not.\n\n### Fundamental Rules\n1. **The Primary Rule:**\n   - *The candidate **writes** the UTME.* (Singular subject $\\rightarrow$ singular verb).\n   - *The candidates **write** the UTME.* (Plural subject $\\rightarrow$ plural verb).\n\n2. **Compound Subjects with \"AND\":**\n   - Two distinct subjects joined by *and* take a **plural verb**:\n     * *Musa and Ade **are** studying in the library.*\n   - **Exception (Singular Notion / Compound Idea):** When two items connected by *and* represent a single combined dish, entity, or idea, they take a **singular verb**:\n     * *Bread and butter **is** my favourite breakfast.*\n     * *Rice and beans **is** delicious.*\n     * *Slow and steady **wins** the race.*\n\n---\n\n## Parenthetical Interveners and Quasi-Coordinators\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Identify modifying parenthetical expressions: *as well as*, *together with*, *along with*, *accompanied by*, *in addition to*, *no less than*, *including*.\n> - Ensure the verb agrees **ONLY with the original head subject preceding the parenthetical phrase**.\n\n> ⚠️ **WAEC & JAMB Chief Examiner Trap:**\n> Examiners deliberately place a plural noun right before the verb inside a parenthetical phrase to trap candidates!\n> - *The principal, as well as the teachers, **is** (NOT are) attending the conference.*\n>   - *Head Subject:* The principal (Singular).\n>   - *Parenthetical modifier:* as well as the teachers.\n>   - *Verb:* **is**.\n> - *The soldiers, accompanied by the commander, **were** (NOT was) ambushed.*\n>   - *Head Subject:* The soldiers (Plural).\n>   - *Verb:* **were**.\n\n---\n\n## The Rule of Proximity with Correlative Conjunctions\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Apply the Rule of Proximity with *Either... or*, *Neither... nor*, *Not only... but also*.\n> - Ensure the verb agrees with the subject **closest (nearest) to it**.\n\n1. **Either... or / Neither... nor:**\n   - *Neither the teacher nor the **students were** present.* (Closest subject: students $\\rightarrow$ plural).\n   - *Neither the students nor the **teacher was** present.* (Closest subject: teacher $\\rightarrow$ singular).\n   - *Either the driver or his **passengers are** liable.* (Closest subject: passengers $\\rightarrow$ plural).\n\n2. **\"Many a\" Construction:**\n   - *Many a* is always followed by a **singular countable noun** and takes a **singular verb**:\n     * *Many a student **has** failed to read the instructions.* (NOT have).\n     * *Many a soldier **dies** in battle.* (NOT die).\n"
