    "summary_60s": "Law of demand: ceteris paribus, higher price leads to lower quantity demanded. Law of supply: higher price leads to higher quantity supplied. Equilibrium is where Qd = Qs. Price elasticity of demand Ed = (%ΔQd) / (%ΔP). Maximum price (price ceiling) causes shortage; minimum price (price floor) causes surplus.",
    "key_formulas": "Ed = (ΔQ / ΔP) × (P₁ / Q₁)\nEquilibrium: Qd = Qs\nCross elasticity Exy = (%ΔQx) / (%ΔPy) [Exy > 0 substitutes, Exy < 0 complements]\nIncome elasticity Ey = (%ΔQ) / (%ΔY) [Ey > 0 normal good, Ey < 0 inferior good]",
    "pro_tips_95": "A change in PRICE causes a MOVEMENT ALONG the demand curve (change in quantity demanded). A change in ANY OTHER FACTOR (income, taste, population) causes a SHIFT OF the entire curve (change in demand)!",
    "syllabus_objectives": "Candidates should be able to: 1. Distinguish between change in demand and change in quantity demanded. 2. Calculate point and arc price elasticity of demand and supply. 3. Solve algebraic market equilibrium equations. 4. Analyze economic impacts of price ceilings and price floors.",
    "updated_at": "Official 2026/2027 Syllabus Masterclass",
    "content": "# THEORY OF DEMAND, SUPPLY AND PRICE DETERMINATION\n\n## Law of Demand & Shift vs Movement Along the Demand Curve\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - State the Law of Demand and outline underlying assumptions (*ceteris paribus*).\n> - Differentiate between movement along a demand curve and shifts of the curve.\n> - Identify abnormal / exceptional demand curves (Veblen goods, Giffen goods).\n\n### 1. The Law of Demand\nThe **Law of Demand** states that, *ceteris paribus* (all other determining factors remaining constant), **the higher the price of a commodity, the lower the quantity demanded**; and conversely, the lower the price, the higher the quantity demanded.\n\n$$\\text{Demand Equation: } Q_d = a - bP \\quad (b > 0)$$\n\n### 2. Crucial Distinction: Movement vs Shift\n\n| Condition | Cause | Graphical Representation | Terminology |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Movement Along Curve** | Change in the **commodity's own price** alone | Point slides up (contraction) or down (expansion) along existing curve | **Change in Quantity Demanded** |\n| **Shift of Entire Curve** | Change in non-price factors (**Income, Tastes, Population, Substitutes**) | Entire curve moves rightward (increase) or leftward (decrease) | **Change in Demand** |\n\n---\n\n## Price Elasticity of Demand (PED / $E_d$)\n\n> 🎯 **Official Syllabus Objectives for this Section:**\n> Candidates must be able to:\n> - Calculate price elasticity of demand using formula: $E_d = \\frac{\\% \\Delta Q_d}{\\% \\Delta P}$.\n> - Classify elasticity into perfectly elastic, elastic, unitary, inelastic, and perfectly inelastic.\n\n$$E_d = \\frac{\\text{Percentage Change in Quantity Demanded}}{\\text{Percentage Change in Price}} = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta P} \\times \\frac{P_1}{Q_1}$$\n\n| Numerical Value | Degree of Elasticity | Economic Meaning | Typical Commodity Examples |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **$E_d = 0$** | **Perfectly Inelastic** | Quantity demanded does not respond at all to price changes | Life-saving medication (Insulin) |\n| **$0 < E_d < 1$** | **Inelastic** | $% \\Delta Q_d < \\% \\Delta P$; quantity changes by a smaller proportion | Basic food staples (Garri, Salt, Water) |\n| **$E_d = 1$** | **Unitary Elastic** | $% \\Delta Q_d = \\% \\Delta P$; total revenue remains constant | Hypothetical benchmark |\n| **$1 < E_d < \\infty$** | **Elastic** | $% \\Delta Q_d > \\% \\Delta P$; quantity changes substantially | Luxury items, goods with close substitutes |\n| **$E_d = \\infty$** | **Perfectly Elastic** | Infinite change in demand at a specific price | Perfect competition products |\n"
